+`invariant_exemptions` switches one off for one vector, with a reason that is
+printed on every run. An invariant that cannot be switched off gets deleted the
+first time a legitimate shape trips it, and a deleted invariant protects nothing.
+
+**An exemption is not free text, and three separate checks refuse one.** All of
+this is ENFORCED in `admit.go` / `exemption.go` and reported on every run; the
+list below is a description of the code, not an aspiration.
+
+1. **THE GROUNDING RULE.** An exemption is admissible only where **the reference
+   oracle ITSELF violates the invariant**. That is asserted, never assumed: the
+   exempted invariant is re-run against the schedule **this vector's own capture
+   recorded**, honouring this vector's `unrecorded_fields`, and must come back
+   `VIOLATED` there. Four verdicts:
+
+   | verdict | meaning | outcome |
+   |---|---|---|
+   | `GROUNDED` | the recorded schedule violates it | admissible |
+   | `UNDETERMINED-ON-THE-RECORD` | this vector's own `unrecorded_fields` withdrew a cell the invariant reads, so the record is **silent** | **admitted and REPORTED**, counted apart from `GROUNDED` because it is not evidence either way (finding T225-F1) |
+   | `DECORATION` | the invariant **holds outright** on the oracle's own numbers, with no assertion withheld | **INADMISSIBLE, exit 2** |
+   | `NOT-EVALUABLE` | the invariant has nothing to assert here at all, with nothing withdrawn | **INADMISSIBLE, exit 2** |
+
+   A fifth, `SCHEDULE-UNREADABLE` (the recorded rows do not parse into a
+   schedule), is also **INADMISSIBLE**. An exemption whose grounding nothing
+   could check is not an exemption that was checked.
+
+2. **THE CITATION** (finding T222-F4). The whole argument for admitting an
+   exemption is that **the oracle was observed to behave that way**, so the
+   exemption must cite the observation — and the harness **mints** the citation
+   rather than reading prose, so it can check that it RESOLVES. Three components,
+   all refused on failure: this vector's `provenance.capture_ref` must name a
+   committed capture artefact that **exists in this repository** and is non-empty;
+   its `provenance.capture_case_id` must **occur inside that artefact's bytes** (a
+   capture is a bundle, and a case id that names no case in it cites a page that
+   is not in the book); and the record's own sentence — the exempted invariant
+   re-run against the captured schedule — must be non-empty. **A hand-authored
+   vector therefore cannot exempt an invariant at all**, because it cites no
+   capture and has not observed the oracle. Every resolved citation is printed
+   with its artefact, its byte size and the offset the case id was found at.
+
+3. **THE PORT CONJUNCT.** The exempted invariant is ALSO run against the schedule
+   the **implementation returned**, and the result is printed beside every
+   exemption. It is **REPORTED, never refused** — a port that satisfies an
+   invariant the oracle violates has diverged, and a divergence is the cell diff's
+   finding, not a second admissibility verdict that could disagree with it.
+
+**The exemption counts are PINNED.** `.softhouse/conformance.sh` compares the
+run's exempted / declared / `GROUNDED` / `UNDETERMINED-ON-THE-RECORD` /
+`UNGROUNDED` figures against `EXEMPTION_PIN_*` **for equality**, so the corpus
+cannot drift in either direction — adding OR removing an exemption is exit 2
+until the pin is edited in the same commit. Promoting a vector that exempts an
+invariant means editing that pin, deliberately, where a reviewer sees it.
